hints:
Move constructor: steal both members, then hollow out the source so its destructor has nothing to free.
```cpp
IntBuffer(IntBuffer&& o) noexcept : data_(o.data_), n_(o.n_) {
    o.data_ = nullptr;
    o.n_ = 0;
}
```
Forget the two `o.` lines and ASan reports a double-free at the end of the first test.

Move assignment must first release what `this` already owns, and must survive `a = std::move(a)` through an alias:
```cpp
IntBuffer& operator=(IntBuffer&& o) noexcept {
    if (this != &o) {
        delete[] data_;
        data_ = o.data_;  n_ = o.n_;
        o.data_ = nullptr; o.n_ = 0;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

Shorter alternative, move-and-swap: move-construct a temporary from the source (that empties it), swap the temporary into `this`, and let the temporary's destructor free your old buffer.
```cpp
IntBuffer& operator=(IntBuffer&& o) noexcept {
    IntBuffer tmp(std::move(o));   // o is now empty: your move ctor did that
    std::swap(data_, tmp.data_);
    std::swap(n_, tmp.n_);
    return *this;                  // tmp dies here, taking our old buffer with it
}
```
A bare `std::swap` with `o` (no temporary) also works, but then `o` walks away holding your old buffer instead of being empty, and the tests expect empty. Either way, both move operations must be `noexcept`, or the `std::vector` identity test fails: without the promise, `vector` copies on reallocation.
